<TopicPage slug="subjects/data-science/intermediate/exploratory-data-analysis">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) is <strong>looking before modelling</strong> — John Tukey's
    1962 argument that data analysis should begin with pictures and questions, not assumptions.
    Its purpose is understanding: what shape is this data, what relationships exist, what is
    weird here? Every strong model choice downstream was usually visible in a good EDA first.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four-pass EDA checklist" widget="equationStep" config={edaCfg} />

  <h2>The toolkit and its habits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Plot types and their jobs:</strong> histograms for shape, boxplots for comparisons, scatter for relationships, line for time, heatmaps for matrices. Choose by question, not decoration.</li>
    <li><strong>Aggregate, then distrust the aggregate:</strong> means hide bimodality; check the histogram before trusting any average (Anscombe's quartet is the permanent reminder).</li>
    <li><strong>Annotate as you go:</strong> EDA notes become the analysis's audit trail — what you checked, what surprised you, what you decided. Future-you is the main audience.</li>
    <li><strong>EDA is iterative with modelling:</strong> model residuals are data too — the best practitioners loop EDA → model → EDA of residuals until the story is stable.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Tukey, Exploratory Data Analysis (1977); Wickham & Grolemund, R for Data Science; Anscombe (1973).</p>
</TopicPage>
